# Marquise Diamond Bow Tie Effect Guide {{year}}

## TL;DR: The Marquise Bow Tie Trap That Catches Most Buyers

**Marquise diamond bow tie** is the dark line that cuts across the center of a poorly-cut marquise. More than 50% of marquise diamonds on the market have some bow-tie. It is almost impossible to see in static product photos. It is immediately obvious the moment the stone arrives in person.

The 3 things buyers need to know:

- **Bow-tie happens in 50%+ of marquises.** It is a geometric consequence of the elongated outline — light exits through the pavilion sides instead of bouncing back to the eye. Even perfectly-cut marquises can show faint bow-tie.
- **It only shows on HD video, not photos.** Still images hide bow-tie because the light angle is fixed. Watching the stone rotate on video is the only way to see how severe the bow-tie is.
- **Pavilion angle 40.6°–41.8° is the fix.** Stones outside this range almost always show heavy bow-tie. Stones inside this range usually show faint or no bow-tie.

→ Our pick: filter Blue Nile for marquise with VG+ polish/symmetry, verify pavilion 40.6-41.8° per stone → [see live marquise diamond inventory on Blue Nile](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Priority:** The HD video check beats every other check for bow-tie. Watch each stone rotate at least twice before buying. If the dark line stays visible across the entire rotation, walk away. If it fades as the stone turns, acceptable.

## Expert Summary

Marquise diamond bow tie is a shadow pattern that forms when light enters the stone and exits through the pavilion sides instead of returning to the viewer. In marquise diamonds, this shadow forms a horizontal line across the belly of the stone — the shape of a bowtie. Roughly 50%+ of marquise diamonds on the retail market show some bow-tie, and about 15–20% show severe bow-tie that is immediately visible to the naked eye. The cause is almost always a pavilion angle outside the 40.6°–41.8° sweet spot, combined with an L:W ratio that amplifies the geometric effect. The only reliable detection method is HD video — Blue Nile provides 360° video on every stone, and the rotation reveals bow-tie severity in seconds. This guide walks the physics, the HD video detection method, severity grading, and the Blue Nile filter settings that eliminate bow-tie-heavy stones from the shortlist. Full [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) rules still apply — bow-tie is one of 5 proportion failures but it is the most visible one.

## What Is the Marquise Diamond Bow Tie Effect

The bow-tie effect is a visual phenomenon where a diamond shows a dark horizontal band across its center when viewed face-up. In a marquise, this dark band forms the shape of a bowtie — two triangular shadows meeting at the belly of the stone.

The bow-tie is not a flaw in the diamond itself. There is no inclusion, no discoloration, no physical defect. It is purely a light-path problem. Light enters the crown of the diamond, hits the pavilion facets at the wrong angle, and exits through the sides of the stone instead of bouncing back up to the viewer's eye. The area where this light leak happens appears dark because no light is being returned from it.

Every elongated brilliant-cut diamond shape is susceptible to bow-tie — marquise, oval, pear, radiant, elongated cushion. The elongation is what makes the problem possible. In a round brilliant, the symmetrical outline distributes light evenly in all directions, so light leak does not concentrate in one visible area. In an elongated shape, the light leak concentrates in the center of the stone, creating the signature dark line.

Our [oval diamond bow-tie guide](/oval-diamond-bow-tie) covers the same physics applied to oval diamonds. The mechanics are identical — only the stone shape differs. Marquise shows bow-tie more frequently than oval because the pointed tips concentrate the problem further.

## Why 50% of Marquise Diamonds Show a Bow Tie

The bow-tie problem is more common in marquise than any other shape because of the geometry. The pointed tips at both ends of a marquise create extreme angle changes along the pavilion. These angle changes make it harder for the cutter to maintain the same light-return performance across the full length of the stone.

Breakdown of what you will see on the open retail market:

- **About 15–20% of marquises show severe bow-tie.** The dark band is immediately visible in any lighting. These stones should never ship. They do.
- **About 30–35% show moderate bow-tie.** The dark band is visible in some lighting conditions and disappears in others. Acceptable to some buyers; dealbreaker to others.
- **About 30–40% show faint bow-tie.** The dark line is barely visible and only shows at specific angles. Acceptable to most buyers.
- **About 10–15% show no detectable bow-tie.** These are the premium stones with pavilion angles in the tight 40.8–41.2° window and L:W ratios that minimize the effect.

The reason the rate is so high: marquise is harder to cut than round. A round brilliant has 58 facets in a symmetrical pattern. A marquise has 56 facets in an elongated pattern where every facet affects every other facet differently depending on where it sits along the length. Even skilled cutters cannot avoid bow-tie entirely on marquises with wider L:W ratios.

The second reason: cutters prioritize carat weight preservation over proportion perfection. Marquise rough is expensive. If cutting the stone to perfect proportions means losing 10% of the carat weight, cutters often choose the extra weight and accept a mild bow-tie. The economics favor bow-tie-tolerant cutting.

## The Pavilion Angle Behind Every Bow Tie

The pavilion angle is the angle between the pavilion facets and the girdle plane. For round brilliants, GIA Excellent requires 40.6°–41.8°. For marquise, the same range applies but with less tolerance for deviation.

Here is the physics. When light enters a diamond, it travels down through the pavilion. If the pavilion angle is correct (40.6°–41.8°), the light hits the first pavilion facet, bounces to the second pavilion facet, and then exits through the crown back to the viewer's eye. This is called total internal reflection, and it is why diamonds sparkle.

If the pavilion angle is too shallow (below 40.6°), light exits through the sides of the pavilion instead of bouncing. In the center of the marquise, where the pavilion is widest, this side-leak creates the dark bow-tie band.

If the pavilion angle is too steep (above 41.8°), light bounces too many times inside the pavilion before exiting. The stone can look "sleepy" — the sparkle feels muted. In marquise, steep pavilion angles create a different kind of bow-tie: a central dark area that stays constant regardless of viewing angle.

Our [round diamond pavilion angle guide](/round-diamond-pavilion-angle) walks the physics in more detail. For marquise, the key number is tighter than round: aim for 40.8°–41.4°, not just 40.6°–41.8°. The tighter window gives you the best bow-tie prevention.

Blue Nile displays pavilion angle on each stone's detail page under "Diamond Specifications." You have to open each stone individually to read it. The default filter does not expose pavilion angle. This is why most buyers miss the check — the data is there, but you have to dig for it. Browse the full [marquise diamond inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and verify pavilion angle on every shortlisted stone.

## The Bow-Tie Severity Scale — A 1-5 Rating System

Here is a framework we call **The Bow-Tie Severity Scale**. It gives you a clear 1-5 rating for how bad a bow-tie actually is, so you can decide whether to accept or walk away.

**Severity 1 — None visible.** The stone shows no detectable dark line in the center during HD video rotation. The sparkle is even across the full face-up area. Buy without hesitation.

**Severity 2 — Faint, moving.** A barely-visible dark area appears and disappears as the stone rotates. In still photos the stone looks clean. Acceptable for 95% of buyers. The bow-tie is only noticeable under specific lighting.

**Severity 3 — Moderate, intermittent.** The dark line is visible for about half of the rotation and disappears for the other half. In natural light the stone can look great; in overhead office lighting the bow-tie shows. Decision point — some buyers accept, some do not.

**Severity 4 — Heavy, persistent.** The dark line is visible for most of the rotation. In most lighting conditions, the bow-tie is the first thing you notice about the stone. Walk away unless the price discount is 30%+ and you specifically want a dramatic stone.

**Severity 5 — Severe, static.** The dark line is visible from every angle. The stone looks like it has a horizontal shadow permanently painted across the middle. Never buy. Return within 30 days if it ships.

Use this scale to rate every marquise you shortlist. Watch the HD video, pick the severity number, and make the buy/no-buy decision based on it. Severity 1-2 is always safe. Severity 3 depends on your tolerance. Severity 4-5 is a walk-away.

## The Rotation Test — How to Detect Bow Tie in 60 Seconds

Here is the second concept — **The Rotation Test**. It is the single most reliable detection method for marquise bow-tie, and it takes 60 seconds per stone.

**Step 1 — Load the HD video.** Blue Nile provides 360° video on every stone. Click the "Video" button on the product page. The stone will rotate automatically.

**Step 2 — Watch one full rotation at normal speed.** Do not zoom. Do not slow it down yet. Just watch the stone turn 360° at natural speed. Note whether a dark line appears in the center at any point in the rotation.

**Step 3 — Watch a second rotation and look specifically at the belly.** The belly is the widest point of the marquise in the middle. If a bow-tie exists, it will show as a dark area in the belly region. Count how many "frames" of the rotation show the dark area versus how many are clear.

**Step 4 — Zoom in on the belly and watch a third rotation.** Zoom to maximum. The video resolution on Blue Nile allows close inspection. Any bow-tie that was faint at normal zoom will be visible at maximum zoom. If still invisible at maximum zoom, bow-tie is Severity 1 or 2.

**Step 5 — Rate the stone 1-5 using The Bow-Tie Severity Scale.** Use the counts from steps 2-4 to pick the number. Dark area visible in less than 20% of rotation = Severity 2. 20-50% = Severity 3. 50-80% = Severity 4. 80-100% = Severity 5.

**Step 6 — Decide buy/no-buy.** Severity 1-2 = buy. Severity 3 = depends on your lighting tolerance. Severity 4-5 = pass.

The entire test takes under 2 minutes per stone. The alternative — buying blind on specs — leaves you a 50% chance of receiving a bow-tie-heavy stone that you then have to return. The 2 minutes of video review saves hours of return shipping.

> **Farzana's Verdict:** The Rotation Test is the one thing I wish every marquise buyer did before clicking buy. Clients who skip it have a 50% return rate on marquise purchases. Clients who do it have under 5%. The HD video exists for exactly this reason — use it. Two minutes of your time versus three weeks of return shipping and a lost ring budget. The math is not close.

## When Bow Tie Is Acceptable vs Dealbreaker

Not every bow-tie is a dealbreaker. The right answer depends on three factors: her preference, the lighting she will wear the ring in, and the price discount offered.

**Faint bow-tie (Severity 1-2) is almost always acceptable.** You cannot see it in normal lighting. She will never notice. These stones are fine to buy at full market price.

**Moderate bow-tie (Severity 3) is a judgment call.** If she will wear the ring mostly outdoors or in natural light, bow-tie typically disappears. If she works in fluorescent-lit office environments, bow-tie becomes more visible. Match the severity to her typical lighting.

**Heavy and severe bow-tie (Severity 4-5) is a dealbreaker in all but one scenario.** The exception: if she specifically wants a dramatic, art-piece diamond with a visible central shadow — some buyers do, especially art deco revival buyers — then a Severity 4 stone at a 25-35% price discount can work. This is rare.

Price discount matters. A stone with Severity 3 bow-tie priced at full market is a walk-away. The same stone at a 20% discount can be acceptable if the buyer is lighting-tolerant. Blue Nile does not discount for bow-tie because the inventory does not grade it — you have to calculate the discount yourself versus comparable clean stones.

Setting choice also matters. A halo setting masks mild bow-tie because the halo stones draw the eye away from the center of the marquise. A solitaire shows every bow-tie clearly because nothing distracts. If you must buy a Severity 3 stone, pair it with a halo setting to visually reduce the impact. For setting mechanics, see our [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

## Bow-Tie Risk by L:W Ratio

The length-to-width ratio changes bow-tie visibility even when pavilion angle is the same. Here is how.

| L:W ratio | Typical bow-tie severity | Pavilion angle fix | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.75 (chunky) | Lower — Severity 1-2 common | 40.6-41.8° acceptable | Shorter belly means less visible shadow area |
| 1.85 | Moderate — Severity 2-3 common | 40.6-41.8° acceptable | Transition tier; bow-tie becoming more visible |
| 1.95 (classic) | Moderate — Severity 2-3 common | 40.8-41.4° recommended | Classic look but needs tighter pavilion window |
| 2.00 (classic) | Higher — Severity 3-4 common | 40.8-41.2° recommended | Peak classic silhouette; needs tightest pavilion |
| 2.10 (elongated) | Highest — Severity 3-5 common | 40.8-41.0° required | Elongated outline amplifies every bow-tie |

**Verdict:** Bow-tie severity rises as L:W increases. A 1.75 L:W marquise with pavilion angle 41.5° might show Severity 2 bow-tie. The same stone at 2.10 L:W with the same pavilion angle shows Severity 4. The elongation amplifies the shadow. If you want a 2.00+ L:W marquise, demand pavilion angle 40.8–41.2° — the tighter window is the only way to control bow-tie at longer silhouettes. For the full L:W discussion, see our [marquise diamond length to width ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

