## Lab vs Natural Bow Tie Rate

Lab-grown marquise diamonds show bow-tie at nearly the same rate as natural — around 50%. The cutting houses that produce lab marquise are the same houses that produce natural marquise, and they use the same equipment and standards.

The small difference: lab inventory shows slightly higher average pavilion angle deviation because cutters are more willing to maximize finished weight at the cost of ideal proportions. Lab rough is cheaper per carat, so the economics favor weight preservation. In practice, about 55% of lab marquises show some bow-tie versus about 50% of natural.

The practical implication: when buying lab, apply the same bow-tie detection methods. Watch the HD video. Rate the severity. Walk away from Severity 4-5 stones even at the discounted lab price. A bad-looking lab marquise is still a bad-looking marquise.

The advantage: lab prices allow more flexibility. If you have a $2,500 budget for a 1-carat natural marquise, you might feel pressure to compromise on bow-tie to stay in budget. The same $2,500 budget in lab buys a 1.5-2 carat stone with room to filter aggressively on bow-tie. Lab buyers should hold out for Severity 1-2 stones only — the inventory depth makes it practical.

> **Farzana's Verdict:** The lab vs natural bow-tie question is less about the stones and more about buyer psychology. Natural buyers often rationalize accepting a Severity 3 bow-tie because the next clean stone costs 20% more and feels out of reach. Lab buyers at the same budget tier have 3× the inventory to choose from, so they can hold out for Severity 1-2 without changing budget. If budget is tight and you want a clean marquise, the lab path is the honest answer.

## Marquise Bow Tie vs Oval, Pear, Radiant — Comparison

Bow-tie is not unique to marquise. Every elongated brilliant-cut shape has some risk. Here is the comparison.

| Shape | Bow-tie rate | Typical severity | Why | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Marquise | 50%+ | 2-4 common | Pointed tips amplify shadow concentration | Highest-risk shape; HD video critical |
| Oval | 50%+ | 2-3 common | Belly wider than marquise; shadow spreads less | High risk but shadow less sharp |
| Pear | 30% | 2 most common | Only one pointed tip; symmetry breaks shadow | Lower risk than marquise or oval |
| Elongated cushion | 25% | 1-2 common | Modified brilliant pavilion; different geometry | Low risk; cushion forgives more |
| Radiant | 20% | 1-2 common | Rectangular outline; different pavilion facet count | Lowest risk among elongated shapes |

**Verdict:** Marquise and oval are the two highest-risk shapes for bow-tie. Pear is in the middle. Elongated cushion and radiant are low-risk. If bow-tie is a dealbreaker for you, radiant or elongated cushion gives the elongated look with the lowest risk. See our [oval diamond bow tie guide](/oval-diamond-bow-tie) for the oval-specific breakdown, the [round diamond vs marquise](/round-diamond-vs-marquise) elongation trade-off, and the [oval vs marquise diamond](/oval-vs-marquise-diamond) comparison for a shape-by-shape bow-tie discussion.

## Setting Choices That Mask or Reveal Bow Tie

Setting choice affects bow-tie visibility. Here is what works and what does not.

**Halo settings mask bow-tie.** The ring of small diamonds around the center stone draws the eye away from the belly of the marquise. A Severity 3 bow-tie that would be obvious in a solitaire often looks like Severity 2 in a halo. If you must buy a Severity 3 stone, choose a halo. Specific example from current Blue Nile inventory: the [Falling Edge Pavé Diamond Halo in 14K Yellow Gold at $2,470](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/falling-edge-pave-diamond-halo-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-311115?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a working halo mounting for a 1-carat marquise with faint bow-tie.

**Three-stone settings mask bow-tie.** The two flanker stones (typically trillions, rounds, or pears) create visual complexity on either side of the center marquise. The eye reads the whole trio instead of focusing on the marquise center. Bow-tie visibility drops by about one Severity level. A current example: the [Marquise Cut Diamond Three Stone Engagement Ring in 14K White Gold at $1,970](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/marquise-cut-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-315103?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses marquise flankers that visually distract from any faint bow-tie in the center stone.

**Solitaire settings reveal bow-tie.** Nothing distracts the eye from the marquise. Every bow-tie shows at its full severity. If you buy a Severity 1-2 stone, solitaire is fine — the [Solitaire Engagement Ring With Pavé Basket in 14K Yellow Gold at $2,260](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/solitaire-engagement-ring-with-pave-basket-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-311230?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a clean mounting that shows a Severity 1 marquise at its best. If the stone is Severity 3+, solitaire makes it worse.

**Bezel settings reduce light return by 5-8%.** This matters for bow-tie because reduced overall brilliance makes the dark central area more visible by contrast. A Severity 2 stone in a bezel can look like Severity 3. Avoid bezel settings if bow-tie is a concern.

**Pavé bands do not mask bow-tie.** The pavé is on the band, not around the center stone. It does not change what the eye sees in the belly of the marquise. The [Milgrain Lace Pavé Diamond Engagement Ring in 14K White Gold](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/milgrain-lace-pave-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-item-315055?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a beautiful pavé band mounting but will not hide a bow-tie — only mask it if paired with a Severity 1-2 marquise.

For complete setting selection, see our [marquise cut diamond pillar guide](/marquise-cut-diamond). For the full proportion framework that drives bow-tie, see our [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

