Filter Blue Nile for 1.00-1.24 ct GIA stones with VG+ polish and symmetry. Run The Rotation Test on every shortlisted stone and reject anything Severity 3+. Pair the winning stone with a halo setting as insurance in case your Rotation Test rated it too generously. → [See the 1-carat marquise shortlist on Blue Nile](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.24&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the lab-grown value buyer**
Filter lab marquise 1.5-2 carat with VG+ polish and symmetry. Lab inventory depth lets you be strict — accept only Severity 1-2 stones. The lower price per carat means you can walk away from 10 Severity 3 stones and still afford the Severity 1 winner. → [See 1.5 carat lab marquise diamonds](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.50&CaratTo=1.99&IsLab=true&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the statement-size buyer (2+ carat)**
Bow-tie risk is highest at 2+ carat. Narrow the pavilion filter to 40.8-41.2° (tightest window) and L:W 1.90-2.00 (classic range). Verify every shortlisted stone on HD video at maximum zoom. If budget allows natural, split-cut sources (Blue Nile Astor or similar branded cuts) are the safest. → [See 2 carat GIA marquise diamonds](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=2.00&CaratTo=2.49&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the bow-tie-tolerant buyer who prioritizes carat weight**
If she specifically wants the biggest possible marquise and will tolerate mild bow-tie, filter for 2-3 carat lab marquise and accept up to Severity 3. Pair with a halo setting to visually reduce severity by one level. The combination buys you a visually larger stone than the strict-filter path at the same budget. → [See 2-3 carat lab marquise options](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=2.00&CaratTo=3.00&IsLab=true&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the bow-tie effect on a marquise diamond?

The bow-tie effect is a dark horizontal shadow that appears across the center of a marquise diamond when light exits through the pavilion sides instead of bouncing back to the viewer. It looks like a dark bowtie shape across the belly of the stone. About 50% of marquise diamonds show some bow-tie.

### Do all marquise diamonds have a bow-tie?

Not all, but most. About 50-55% of marquise diamonds on the retail market show some bow-tie. About 10-15% show no detectable bow-tie — these are the premium stones with pavilion angles in the tight 40.8-41.2° window. The remaining 85-90% range from faint to severe.

### How do I check for bow-tie on a Blue Nile marquise?

Open the stone's product page on Blue Nile. Click the "Video" button to load the 360° HD video. Watch the stone rotate at least twice at normal zoom, then zoom to maximum and watch a third rotation. If a dark line stays visible across most of the rotation, the stone has heavy bow-tie. If the dark line is barely visible or disappears as the stone turns, bow-tie is mild and acceptable.

### What pavilion angle avoids bow-tie on a marquise?

Pavilion angle 40.8°-41.2° gives the best bow-tie prevention on marquise. The broader 40.6°-41.8° range is acceptable but allows some mild bow-tie. For 2+ carat statement marquises, demand the tighter 40.8-41.2° window — the elongated outline at larger sizes amplifies every bow-tie.

### Can a bow-tie go away over time?

No. The bow-tie is a geometric effect caused by the diamond's proportions. It will look the same tomorrow, next year, and 50 years from now. The only way to eliminate bow-tie is to recut the stone, which costs 15-25% of the stone's value and risks reducing carat weight.

### Is bow-tie visible in diamond photos?

Barely. Still product photos use carefully controlled lighting that minimizes bow-tie visibility. The dark line that shows clearly in HD video rotation often disappears in a static photo. This is why buying marquise on photos alone is dangerous — the stone looks great online and shows bow-tie the moment it arrives.

### How do I rate the severity of a bow-tie?

Use the 1-5 severity scale. Severity 1 = no bow-tie detected on video. Severity 2 = faint bow-tie visible for less than 20% of rotation. Severity 3 = moderate bow-tie visible for 20-50% of rotation. Severity 4 = heavy bow-tie visible for 50-80% of rotation. Severity 5 = severe bow-tie visible across the full rotation.

### At what severity should I walk away from a marquise?

Severity 4 and 5 are walk-aways in nearly all cases. Severity 3 is a judgment call based on her lighting tolerance and your price discount. Severity 1 and 2 are safe to buy. The simplest rule: if the dark line is visible more often than it is hidden during video rotation, pass.

### Does L:W ratio affect bow-tie?

Yes. Longer L:W ratios show bow-tie more severely because the elongated shape amplifies the central shadow. A marquise at L:W 1.75 shows bow-tie less than the same proportions at L:W 2.10. If you want a 2.00+ L:W marquise, demand tighter pavilion angle (40.8-41.2°) to compensate for the amplification.

### Does a halo setting hide bow-tie?

Partially. The ring of small diamonds around the center stone draws the eye away from the belly where bow-tie shows. A Severity 3 bow-tie that would be obvious in a solitaire looks more like Severity 2 in a halo. The bow-tie is still there — it just gets less visual attention.

### Are lab-grown marquises more likely to have bow-tie?

Slightly. About 55% of lab marquises show bow-tie versus about 50% of natural. The difference is small and comes from lab cutters prioritizing finished carat weight over ideal proportions because lab rough is cheaper. Apply the same HD video check regardless of lab or natural.

### Is pear-shaped diamond bow-tie worse than marquise?

No. Pear shows bow-tie in about 30% of stones versus 50%+ for marquise. The pear's single pointed tip and wider belly spread the shadow less dramatically than the marquise's two tips. Pear is the lower-bow-tie choice if you want an elongated fancy shape.

### Can I return a marquise ring if the bow-tie is worse in person than it looked online?

Yes, during the return window. Blue Nile offers 30-day returns. If the stone arrives and the bow-tie is more severe than you expected from the HD video, document it with your own video, contact customer service, and ship it back within 30 days. The HD video verification before purchase is still the better path — avoiding the return beats processing one.

### What is the safest marquise L:W for low bow-tie?

L:W 1.75-1.85 has the lowest bow-tie risk. The chunkier outline minimizes the central shadow area. If bow-tie is a top concern and you have no strong preference for a longer silhouette, the 1.75-1.85 range is the lowest-risk choice.

